(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-bacterial-infections-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cutaneous Bacterial Infections Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which conditions are named in the differential for bullous impetigo?","choices":["Varicella, insect bites, herpes simplex, tinea corporis and scabies","Deep vein thrombosis, discoid lupus and cellulitis of the limb","Cystic acne, folliculitis and hidradenitis suppurativa","Thermal burn, allergic contact dermatitis, and herpes simplex or zoster"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — non-bullous impetigo has a different differential list. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"How does acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis present after impetigo?","choices":["Gradual weight loss, painless jaundice, pale stools and dark urine over several weeks","Fever with a sandpaper rash, strawberry tongue and circumoral pallor within days","Migratory joint pain, a new heart murmur and subcutaneous nodules over the elbows","Sudden oedema, haematuria with smoky or tea-coloured urine, proteinuria and hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, especially in three to seven year olds. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which tests may be used in acute paronychia and what does each exclude?","choices":["Wood's lamp for coral-red fluorescence, potassium hydroxide for dermatophytes, biopsy for malignancy","Blood cultures for bacteraemia, creatine phosphokinase for muscle damage, imaging for osteomyelitis","Nasal swab for staphylococcal carriage, plucked hair for dermatophytes, biopsy for follicular disease","Gram stain and culture for the bacterial cause, potassium hydroxide for candida, Tzanck smear for herpetic whitlow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is usually a clinical diagnosis, so testing is directed rather than routine. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"Describe the physical findings of a carbuncle.","choices":["Several loculated abscesses, superficial pustules, necrotic plugs and sieve-like draining openings","A single firm tender nodule with one opening and a surrounding zone of erythema and swelling","Nodules, sinus tracts and meshlike scarring in the axilla or the perineum","A well-demarcated raised plaque with red streaks extending toward a lymph node"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a deeper infection made of interconnecting furuncles in several follicles. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What are the examination findings of cellulitis?","choices":["Tense bullae with purple fluid and skin that has become insensate over the affected area","All four cardinal signs of inflammation, most often on one lower leg, with borders neither raised nor demarcated","All four cardinal signs of inflammation, most often bilateral, with a sharply raised and demarcated edge","Painless erythema with scaling at the border, most often over intertriginous skin surfaces"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral disease is described as almost never occurring. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"What is the hallmark lesion of acne vulgaris on physical examination?","choices":["The comedone, either open (blackhead) or closed (whitehead)","The pustule, sitting on an erythematous base around a central hair","The nodule, deep-seated and arising in several adjacent follicles","The papule, small and inflamed without any surface keratin plug"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acne is polymorphic, but the comedone is what defines it. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the microbiology of necrotizing fasciitis, as Professor Jaquith described it?","choices":["Always methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus","Polymicrobial from mixed flora, or group A Streptococcus","Always monomicrobial Staphylococcus aureus","Always Pseudomonas aeruginosa from water exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — aerobic or anaerobic. She introduced it as the flesh-eating bacteria. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:10:15"},{"q":"Which shaving advice is given for pseudofolliculitis barbae?","choices":["Continue the current routine and treat each papule with a topical antibiotic","Stop shaving if possible, use clean razors, and avoid lift-and-cut razor systems","Shave daily against the grain with a multi-blade razor to cut hairs below the surface","Shave weekly with an electric razor set to the closest possible cutting length"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — mild razor angles and single or at most double blades are preferred. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which measure is recommended when methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is a concern in a drained furuncle?","choices":["Start linezolid empirically before any material is obtained","Swab the nasal vestibule of every household contact first","Defer drainage until the culture result becomes available","Culture the material obtained at incision and drainage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and give endocarditis prophylaxis to at-risk patients. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is a furuncle?","choices":["An infection of the upper dermis extending into superficial skin lymphatics","A deep-seated abscess of a hair follicle and the adjacent subcutaneous tissue","An inflammation of the follicle wall and ostia forming a superficial pustule","A collection of purulent material seeded by traumatic inoculation of bacteria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus is the commonest organism. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is Professor Jaquith's simplified default when methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is suspected?","choices":["Cephalexin alone at a higher dose","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole double strength alone","Intravenous vancomycin as an outpatient","Cephalexin plus trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — double strength, and the combination is broad enough to cover before susceptibilities return, which then confirm. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 23:31"},{"q":"What should be suspected when acne scarring seems out of proportion to the severity of lesions?","choices":["An underlying immunodeficiency impairing the healing of each lesion","Patient manipulation of the lesions through picking or squeezing","An undiagnosed hyperandrogenic state driving deeper inflammation","Resistance of Cutibacterium acnes to the prescribed antimicrobial"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients and families are educated about that risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which specimens are taken in serious cellulitis?","choices":["Perilesional tissue for direct immunofluorescence","A potassium hydroxide preparation of the leading edge","Blood cultures and a skin punch biopsy","A superficial swab of the intact affected skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside a complete blood count and creatine phosphokinase. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Where does acne vulgaris rank among skin diseases in the United States?","choices":["It is the second most common, affecting about 40% of Americans in their lifetime","It is the most common in adolescents only, affecting about 20% of adults","It is uncommon overall, affecting fewer than 10% of Americans in their lifetime","It is the most common skin disease, affecting about 80% of Americans in their lifetime"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It usually begins with the onset of puberty. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the definitive procedure for an abscess that will not drain?","choices":["Surgical incision and drainage, covered in detail in a later course","Wide excision of the affected area under general anaesthesia","Aspiration with a fine needle under ultrasound guidance","Observation with warm soaks until it drains on its own"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the aim is to eradicate infection and prevent recurrence. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Why will antibiotics not work on the devitalised area in a cellulitic limb?","choices":["The organism in devitalised tissue is always methicillin resistant","Devitalised tissue destroys antibiotic molecules on contact","Oral antibiotics never reach the subcutaneous tissue at all","It is not perfused, so the drug never reaches the site"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — tense, cyanotic, necrotic, bronzed or blanched tissue needs debridement. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"What is a carbuncle?","choices":["A furuncle that has become secondarily infected with yeast","A furuncle that has failed to respond to oral antibiotics","Two or more confluent furuncles with separate heads","A single furuncle larger than five millimetres across"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — interconnecting furuncles arising in several hair follicles. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is the diagnostic finding in erythrasma?","choices":["Coral-red fluorescence under a Wood's lamp","Green fluorescence under a Wood's lamp","Branching hyphae on a potassium hydroxide mount","Gram-positive cocci in chains on a Gram stain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That is what separates it from tinea and candidiasis clinically. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"What is the natural course of hot tub folliculitis?","choices":["Chronic and relapsing for months after a single exposure","Resolving only after the affected follicles are drained","Usually self-limiting, clearing in two to ten days","Progressive without oral ciprofloxacin in every patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ciprofloxacin is reserved for widespread or resistant cases. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotic is described as the drug of choice for impetigo in children?","choices":["Dicloxacillin","Erythromycin","Cephalexin","Doxycycline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is used where there is penicillin allergy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Where and after what does Professor Jaquith say she sees folliculitis most often?","choices":["The groin, after shaving","The scalp, after using hair oils","The lower legs, after swimming in fresh water","The face, after using an occlusive moisturiser"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — hot tubs come second, and hot tub folliculitis is the Pseudomonas one. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 17:37"},{"q":"Professor Jaquith treats a small-surface-area non-purulent cellulitis as an outpatient. What follow-up does she insist on, and why?","choices":["No routine follow-up unless the patient deteriorates","Return in 72 hours for repeat blood cultures","Return in 24 to 48 hours, because it spreads so fast","Return in one week, once the course is complete"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — to confirm the antibiotic is working. That interval is the safety net on outpatient treatment. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:01:43"},{"q":"Which oral agents are named for folliculitis when methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is cultured or suspected?","choices":["Doxycycline, minocycline or sarecycline","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, ciprofloxacin or linezolid","Cephalexin, dicloxacillin or amoxicillin-clavulanate","Penicillin V, clindamycin or erythromycin base"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin and dicloxacillin cover the methicillin-sensitive organism. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which layer does erysipelas involve, and which organism causes it?","choices":["The upper dermis and superficial cutaneous lymphatics, most often Group A streptococcus","The deeper dermis and subcutaneous tissue, most often Staphylococcus aureus","The superficial epidermis only, most often Streptococcus pyogenes or staphylococci","The fascia surrounding muscle and nerve, most often a polymicrobial mixture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a superficial cellulitis of the dermal layer. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"On the ladder she flagged, what is added for MODERATE acne with pustules?","choices":["Azelaic acid used on its own as a single topical","A topical retinoid, an ORAL antibiotic, and benzoyl peroxide","A topical retinoid and benzoyl peroxide, with no oral agent","Oral isotretinoin used as the sole agent from the outset"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the severe tier is the same three, or oral isotretinoin instead. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 15:25"},{"q":"Which preventive measures are advised in hidradenitis suppurativa?","choices":["Avoid heat, cleanse daily with antibacterial soap or benzoyl peroxide wash, lose weight, stop smoking","Avoid all water contact, apply a barrier ointment daily, gain weight and use an occlusive dressing","Shower immediately after any hot tub use and monitor the disinfectant levels in the water","Avoid sharing towels or razors and isolate from school until treatment has been underway"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, along with avoiding constrictive clothing and considering laser hair removal. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotics are used for furuncles and carbuncles when methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is cultured or suspected?","choices":["Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, clindamycin or doxycycline","Dicloxacillin, cephalexin or amoxicillin-clavulanate","Penicillin V, erythromycin or clarithromycin","Ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin or moxifloxacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy for the sensitive organism is dicloxacillin or cephalexin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How should laboratory tests and imaging be sequenced in suspected necrotizing fasciitis?","choices":["They should be repeated at forty-eight hours, because early results are usually uninformative","They should not delay surgical intervention, because this is a surgical emergency with high mortality","They should be completed and reviewed before surgery, because the findings determine the incision","They should replace surgery where imaging is negative, because operating carries its own risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blood count, chemistry, arterial blood gas, urinalysis and cultures are all sent. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"What is the primary portal of entry for impetigo?","choices":["A hair follicle already inflamed by shaving or occlusive clothing","Traumatic inoculation of bacteria deep into the dermal tissues","Minor superficial breaks in the skin such as cuts and bug bites","An occluded apocrine duct that ruptures into the surrounding dermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. No predisposing lesion is identified in most patients. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which two combination topical products are named for reducing antibiotic resistance in acne?","choices":["Cleocin T (clindamycin with salicylic acid) and Erygel (erythromycin with salicylic acid)","Altabax (retapamulin with mupirocin) and Bactroban (mupirocin with benzoyl peroxide)","BenzaClin (clindamycin with benzoyl peroxide) and Benzamycin (erythromycin with benzoyl peroxide)","Differin (adapalene with benzoyl peroxide) and Tazorac (tazarotene with benzoyl peroxide)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both pair a topical antibiotic with benzoyl peroxide. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 24"}]};
